<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/foundational/how-machines-learn">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Artificial Intelligence. You need no prior knowledge —
    no programming, no calculus, no statistics. If you have ever emptied a spam folder, you
    have already met the subject.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say, in one sentence, what it actually means for a machine to "learn";</li>
    <li>follow the guess–measure–step loop, and compute one training step by hand;</li>
    <li>name the three kinds of machine learning, with an everyday example of each;</li>
    <li>explain why a model that scores perfectly in training can fail in the real world — and how to catch it;</li>
    <li>describe how neural networks and deep learning scale that same loop up.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every new term is explained the moment it appears. Take your time — one idea per
    paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a problem no rules can solve</h2>
  <p>
    For most of computing's history, a program was answers written by a human. Want a
    machine to add two numbers? A person writes the rule. But some problems refuse to be
    written as rules. Spam — unwanted junk email — is the classic. Nobody can write the
    rule for what spam looks like, because spam is "whatever spammers decide this month".
    Try listing the signs: odd senders, urgency, misspellings. The spammers change tactics
    the moment such a list works.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix was a different way of building software. Instead of writing rules, you write a
    <strong>learning loop</strong>. The machine makes a guess. A <strong>loss
    function</strong> — a formula that measures how wrong the guess was — scores it.
    Calculus supplies the direction of "downhill", called the <strong>gradient</strong>.
    The machine's adjustable <strong>parameters</strong> — the internal numbers being
    tuned — each take a small step that way. Repeat a few million times, and behaviour
    emerges that no one programmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    That loop — guess, measure, step — is <strong>gradient descent</strong>, and in one
    form or another it trains everything from spam filters to ChatGPT-class models. This is
    the deep sense in which machines "learn": not acquiring understanding, but
    systematically reducing a mistake score against data.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the whole of machine learning in one picture — red dots are training steps rolling downhill to the best answer" widget="plotter" config={descendCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> hover along the purple curve to read the loss at any
    parameter value, then trace the red dots from left to right. The first steps are huge and
    each later one is smaller, because every step is the local slope times the learning rate
    and the slope flattens near the bottom. Watch the dots effectively stop around w = 3:
    that is convergence, the best answer this data supports.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each red dot is one training step. The model here has a single parameter
    <code>w</code>; the purple curve is the loss — how wrong the model is — for every
    possible setting of that parameter. Start at w = 0 (badly wrong, loss = 10), measure
    the slope (steeply negative, so step right), and watch the steps shrink as the ground
    flattens. Near the bottom the gradient is almost zero, so the model stops moving:
    <em>converged</em>, at w = 3, the best answer the data supports. Real networks do this
    in millions or billions of dimensions, where "downhill" cannot be drawn, only computed
    — but the geometry is identical, and so is the path: big strides early, tiptoeing at
    the end, each step size set by the <strong>learning rate</strong> (the fixed fraction
    of the slope taken as a step) times the local slope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a machine "learns" by guessing, scoring its wrongness with a
    loss function, and stepping its parameters a little downhill — millions of times. Now:
    where do the examples come from?
  </p>

  <h2>Learning from examples: the supervised recipe</h2>
  <p>
    The dominant form of machine learning is <strong>supervised learning</strong>: you have
    examples with the right answers attached. Emails labelled spam or not-spam. X-rays
    labelled by radiologists. House sales with the prices recorded. The task is to infer
    the mapping from inputs to labels — like a student revising from an answered exam
    paper.
  </p>
  <p>
    Supervised tasks split into two families. <strong>Classification</strong> predicts a
    category: spam or not, cat, dog or hamster. <strong>Regression</strong> predicts a
    number: tomorrow's temperature, a house's price.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two other paradigms complete the map. <strong>Unsupervised learning</strong> gets no
    labels at all — it finds structure: which customers cluster together, which words
    appear together. <strong>Reinforcement learning</strong> is the third: an
    <em>agent</em> acts, receives rewards, and learns which actions pay off — how systems
    learned to play Go and control robots.
  </p>
  <p>
    The prototype of supervised learning is the <strong>decision tree</strong>: a flowchart
    of questions learned from data ("is the email from an unknown sender? is it asking for
    a password?"), where each internal node tests one attribute and each leaf is a
    prediction. Think of the game Twenty Questions, but grown from examples instead of
    played for fun. Trees are beloved for one reason: you can read them.
  </p>
  <p>
    An algorithm like ID3 chooses each split by <strong>information gain</strong> — the
    question that most reduces uncertainty about the answer goes first. A tree that
    perfectly classifies its training data can be grown in seconds, which is both the
    method's power and, as we will see, its trap.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the machine does not "figure out the rules" in any human sense.
  It adjusts numbers to reduce a score. A spam filter has no concept of deception; it has weights that
  happened to distinguish the labelled examples. When the world shifts (new spam tactics, a hospital
  changing scanners), the model does not notice — it must be retrained, and the shift has a name,
  <strong>concept drift</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Generalisation: the only score that matters</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the field's central paradox. You train on the data you have, but you are graded
    on data you do not have. A model judged on its <strong>training data</strong> — the
    examples it learned from — can cheat by memorising. The machine-learning name for this
    is <strong>overfitting</strong>: the model has learned the noise, not the pattern.
  </p>
  <p>
    A decision tree grown to perfection on last year's emails may have leaves like "sender
    contains the letter q" — a rule that fit the sample and will misfire on tomorrow's
    inbox. A student who memorises last year's answers, word for word, fails the new exam.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defence is procedural and simple: split the data. Train on one portion. Evaluate on
    a <strong>validation set</strong> the model never saw, and tune on that. Report final
    performance on a third <strong>test set</strong>, touched exactly once. When data is
    scarce, <strong>k-fold cross-validation</strong> rotates the split k times and
    averages. The tell-tale signature of overfitting is a widening gap: training error
    keeps falling while validation error starts to rise — the moment to stop or to
    simplify.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Regularisation</strong> (penalising model complexity inside the loss itself)
    and <strong>pruning</strong> (cutting a tree's weakest branches) are the standard
    curbs. They work because they encode a philosophical bet: <em>simpler explanations that
    fit the data are more likely to extend to new data</em> — Ockham's razor wearing a loss
    function.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two practical corollaries finish the hygiene. <strong>Data leakage</strong>: information
    from the future or from the test set — a field the model should not legally see, a
    scaler fitted on all the data — inflates results until the model meets reality. It is
    the most common way honest-looking evaluations lie. And <strong>class
    imbalance</strong>: a fraud model that labels everything "legitimate" is 99.9% accurate
    and completely useless, which is why imbalanced problems are scored with precision,
    recall and the <strong>confusion matrix</strong> (a table of what the model got right
    and wrong, by category) rather than raw accuracy.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a model with 99.9% accuracy is nearly perfect. On a fraud
  dataset where fraud is rare, a model that calls everything "legitimate" reaches that score
  while catching zero fraud — which is exactly why imbalanced problems are judged with
  precision, recall and the confusion matrix instead of raw accuracy.</p>

  <h2>Neural networks and deep learning</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>neural network</strong> is layers of simple units, each computing a weighted
    sum of its inputs passed through a soft nonlinearity — loosely inspired by neurons,
    mostly just useful mathematics. One layer of units can only draw simple boundaries. Two
    or more (<strong>hidden layers</strong>) can approximate almost any function — that is
    the <strong>universal approximation theorem</strong>, and it explains both the ambition
    and the difficulty.
  </p>
  <p>
    The difficulty was training: with a million weights, which way is downhill? The answer
    — <strong>backpropagation</strong>, popularised by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams in
    1986 — is gradient descent organised by the chain rule. Compute the error at the
    output. Pass it backwards, layer by layer. Each weight learns its own share of the
    blame. That single algorithm made multilayer networks trainable, and modern deep
    learning is essentially backprop plus scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes learning <em>deep</em> is depth used for <strong>representation
    learning</strong>: early layers learn simple features (edges in an image, syllables in
    text), middle layers combine them (eyes, wheels; phrases), late layers combine those
    (faces, vehicles; sentences) — a hierarchy built from the data rather than designed by
    hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Convolutional networks</strong> (LeCun, late 1980s) hard-wire one insight — a
    feature useful in one corner of an image is useful everywhere — by sliding small
    filters across the whole picture. That slashed the number of parameters and made vision
    practical. The inflection point came from three compounding trends: data (ImageNet's
    roughly 1.2 million labelled images), compute (GPUs, built for games, turned out to be
    gradient machines), and algorithmic tricks. In 2012 a deep convolutional net (AlexNet)
    roughly halved the error rate of the ImageNet vision contest; the field reorganised
    within two years.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same recipe — layers, backprop, scale — now generates text, translates languages
    and folds proteins. The <strong>transformer</strong> architecture behind modern
    language models (2017) is the newest verse: an attention mechanism that lets every word
    in a sequence weigh every other, trained by exactly the guess–measure–step loop you
    computed above, at a scale where the widget's single w becomes a hundred billion
    parameters.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> neural networks are brain simulations. They borrow a word and a
  loose shape from neurons; each unit is just a weighted sum plus a simple nonlinearity, and
  backpropagation has no known biological counterpart. The metaphor is inspiration, not
  mechanism.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three ways to learn (supervised, unsupervised,
    reinforcement), one way to be graded (unseen data), and one scaling trick (layers
    trained by backpropagation). Next, we compute the loop by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>A model you can hold in your head: training by hand</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing demystifies the loop like computing one step yourself. Take the simplest model,
    a line: prediction ŷ = w·x, one parameter w. Training data: two points, (1, 2) and
    (2, 4) — the true relationship is y = 2x. The <strong>loss function</strong> is mean
    squared error: L(w) = ½[(w·1 − 2)² + (w·2 − 4)²].
  </p>
  <p>
    Start at w = 0: predictions are 0 and 0, errors are −2 and −4, loss is ½(4 + 16) = 10 —
    badly wrong, exactly like the first red dot in the plot. Now the gradient:
    dL/dw = (w − 2)·1 + (2w − 4)·2 = 5w − 10. At w = 0 the slope is −10 (steeply downhill
    to the right, as the picture shows).
  </p>
  <p>
    Take one step with learning rate η = 0.1: w ← 0 − 0.1×(−10) = 1.0. Recompute:
    predictions 1 and 2, loss ½(1 + 4) = 2.5. Second step: slope = 5×1 − 10 = −5, so
    w ← 1 + 0.5 = 1.5, loss = ½(0.25 + 1) = 0.625.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each step multiplies the error by (1 − 0.5) — the loss decays geometrically, w glides
    0 → 1 → 1.5 → 1.75 → … toward 2, and the steps shrink because the slope shrinks. That
    arithmetic <em>is</em> the widget, in one dimension. Everything deep about the field is
    this loop repeated: in a neural network there are millions of w's, the gradient is
    computed by backpropagation rather than one derivative, an <strong>epoch</strong> is
    one full pass through the training data, a <strong>batch</strong> is the handful of
    examples each gradient is computed from (stochastic gradient descent), and the loss
    surface is no longer a friendly bowl — but "guess, measure, step" is unchanged, and you
    have now done it once yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What does a loss function measure?</li>
    <li>Training error keeps falling while validation error rises. What is happening, and what should you do?</li>
    <li>A fraud detector labels every transaction "legitimate" and scores 99.9% accuracy. Why is it useless?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) How wrong a model's guess is, on the examples it trains
    against — the score the learning loop tries to drive down. (2) The model is
    overfitting: memorising its training data instead of learning the pattern — so stop or
    simplify, using the validation set as the referee. (3) Because fraud is rare, calling
    everything legitimate scores 99.9% while catching no fraud at all — imbalanced
    problems need precision, recall and the confusion matrix.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A machine "learns" by running the guess-measure-step loop: a loss function scores each guess and the parameters step a little downhill, millions of times.</li>
    <li>Supervised learning infers a mapping from labelled examples — classification for categories, regression for numbers; unsupervised learning finds structure without labels; reinforcement learning learns from rewards.</li>
    <li>Decision trees grow by information gain and are readable — which is also what makes them easy to overfit.</li>
    <li>The only score that matters is on unseen data: validation to tune, a test set touched once, and a widening train-validation gap as the overfitting alarm.</li>
    <li>Raw accuracy lies on imbalanced data; precision, recall and the confusion matrix tell the truth.</li>
    <li>Neural networks are layers of weighted sums trained by backpropagation, and depth earns the name "deep" by learning a hierarchy of features from the data itself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The lessons that follow scale this loop up: the same guess-measure-step arithmetic builds
    neural networks from scratch, classifies and regresses on real datasets, and drives the
    transformers behind modern language models — while the evaluation discipline you just
    practised returns as the field's checklist for bias, safety and honest claims.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The gradient-descent picture hides real subtleties worth knowing. <strong>Loss
    surfaces are not bowls.</strong> The widget's valley has one bottom; real loss surfaces
    in high dimensions have saddle points, plateaus and many minima — remarkably, in deep
    networks most local minima are nearly as good as the global one, so "getting stuck" is
    less of a problem than feared. <strong>The learning rate is the master dial</strong>:
    too large and the steps leap across the valley and diverge; too small and training
    crawls or freezes on a plateau; practical training uses schedules or adaptive methods
    (Adam, 2014) that tune the effective step per parameter. <strong>Stochastic gradient
    descent</strong> computes the gradient on small random batches rather than the whole
    dataset — noisier steps, but thousands of times cheaper per step, and the noise itself
    seems to help generalisation. <strong>Beyond supervised:</strong> the models behind
    modern chat assistants are trained in stages — self-supervised pretraining (predict
    the next word in trillions of tokens, no labels needed), then fine-tuning with human
    feedback, where the loss is a human preference score. And the honest caveats: models
    inherit their datasets' biases (a hiring model learns the past's discrimination as
    signal), can be brittle off-distribution (a panda plus a carefully chosen sticker
    becomes, to the network, a gibbon with high confidence), and cannot tell you what they
    do not know. The discipline for all of it is unchanged from this lesson: every claim
    about an AI system should trace back to a loss function, a dataset and an evaluation on
    unseen data. That discipline — not the hype — is the field.
  </p>
